          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for about six years.",
          "answer": "Gastroesophageal reflux disease, often called GERD or simply reflux, is a condition where stomach contents flow back into the esophagus often enough, or with enough impact, to cause bothersome symptoms or damage to the esophageal lining. Some reflux is normal for everyone; it becomes a disease when it causes significant symptoms or injury. This typically happens when the lower esophageal sphincter, a muscle ring between the esophagus and stomach, relaxes at inappropriate times. This explanation comes from the document \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\".\n\nThe information I have does not classify reflux based on its duration, such as whether a six-year history would be considered chronic. The coding guidelines for reflux disease also state that the duration of the condition does not determine its classification for coding purposes, according to \"Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21 and the esophagitis distinction\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
